rks. This is not a case of one weird rock. We've now identified over 300 Martian meteorites. They all share the same consistent story, and their composition has been verified again and again by rovers analyzing rocks still on Mars. So, the "gas pockets" aren't just weird; they're a perfect, unique match to a planet's atmosphere that we measured robotically. That's not a coincidence. It's a physical sample from Mars, delivered to Earth, and that's a cross-check that requires no faith—just chemistry and physics.
